## 2. The SLA behavior change (Decision 1) — and why

**Policy:** once a solution is proposed (`state = Resolved`, awaiting customer),
the case no longer owes a recurring Infor-authored cadence update. Previously
(v8) these cases were judged for SLA cadence with `end = snapshot`, so a resolved
case sitting idle accrued a **trailing-gap cadence breach** for going stale —
wrong under current policy.

**Chosen: "stop the clock," not "exempt."** A full exemption (`eligible = false`)
was rejected: it would also drop initial-response and pre-resolution cadence
misses out of the denominator, overstating SLA. We only want to stop the
*trailing-idle* penalty, while still judging what happened **before** resolution.

The load-bearing subtlety — surfaced by the adversarial self-critique (§7) — is
that "stop the clock" is **three different clocks**, and conflating them into one
`end` is incorrect. The final design keeps them separate in `computeSlaSop`:

| Clock | Anchor for Solution Proposed | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Cadence trailing gap** | last **Infor** update (`ts[last]`) | Infor owes nothing after its last touch → gap is zero. Using the Infor-only timeline (not last activity of any author) stops a late *customer* reply from fabricating a breach. A real gap *between* pre-resolution Infor updates is still caught by the inter-update loop. |
| **Initial response** | the **snapshot** | A resolved case Infor *never answered* is a genuine missed first response; judging to the snapshot keeps it counted (anchoring to last activity would let an early customer post mask it). |
| **Auto-close countdown** | when the **resolution notes were saved** (`resolved_at_ms`), then last Infor note, then created; clamped ≥ created | ServiceNow's 90-day auto-close starts at resolution. Its "Case Resolved – Reminder N" warning notes (and a late customer reply) must **not** move the clock — see §3 / the user's caveat. |

Net effect: Resolved cases get `sla_due_sop = null` (drop out of
at-risk/overdue/forecast), and `sla_breached` flips false for cases whose *only*
miss was trailing idle — but initial-response and pre-resolution cadence misses
still breach. **SLA % generally rises** (intended).

