## 3. Auto-close anchor = resolution-notes-saved time (Decision 2, revised after a domain caveat)

The initial design anchored the countdown to "last activity (any author)" on the
theory that ServiceNow resets the 90-day clock on any update. A domain correction
from the user — verified against the real export — overrode that: **the clock
starts when the resolution notes are saved, and the system "Case Resolved –
Reminder N" auto-close *warning* notes must not count.**

`resolved_at_ms` is therefore the header timestamp of the **latest journal entry
carrying the `<b>Resolution notes</b>` marker** (`parseResolutionTime` over
`additional_comments`, where ServiceNow writes it — confirmed present on 233/247
Resolved rows, incl. System auto-resolutions). The auto-close reminder notes live
in `work_notes`, carry no marker, and so are ignored automatically. Marker-less
Resolved rows fall back to the **last Infor note** (`parseInforUpdates.lastTs`,
now system-free per §3a), then `created`; the anchor is clamped ≥ created so a
backdated header can't start the clock before the case existed (finding F4).

### 3a. System auto-reminder notes excluded from analyst attribution
The reminder/auto-resolution actor is authored `System  Automatic Reminders
(Infor)` — the `(Infor)` tag made `ANALYST_AUTHOR = /\(Infor\)/i` wrongly count it
as analyst work in `parseInforUpdates` (cadence, `last_infor_update`) and
`parseInteractions` (turn counts), skewing ~141 auto-resolved cases. A new
`SYSTEM_AUTHOR = /^\s*System\b/i` guard (`isInforAnalyst = ANALYST_AUTHOR && !SYSTEM_AUTHOR`)
excludes them from both. This removes false `cadence` breaches on auto-resolved
cases and corrects `analyst_turns` / FCR / multi-touch (numbers shift — intended).

**Modeling assumption (surfaced in the UI + README):** ServiceNow's true auto-close
anchor is instance-configured. We model it as the resolution-notes save time
against the data-as-of snapshot. The page subtitle states this plainly.

